1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,709
Caching runs the full network every N
steps.

2
00:00:04,509 --> 00:00:08,766
In between, it predicts the missing
features from history.

3
00:00:09,016 --> 00:00:13,581
Existing methods apply one prediction rule
to the whole feature.

4
00:00:13,831 --> 00:00:18,546
But is one rule enough for every part of a
feature?

5
00:00:19,846 --> 00:00:23,554
Follow a single feature across the
denoising trajectory.

6
00:00:24,354 --> 00:00:29,040
Some dimensions jump around, and they are
hard to predict.

7
00:00:29,290 --> 00:00:31,833
Others change smoothly, almost linearly.

8
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:35,911
And some curve gently, so higher-order
prediction helps.

9
00:00:36,161 --> 00:00:40,299
These behaviours are mixed together inside
the same vector.

10
00:00:41,099 --> 00:00:44,807
One prediction order cannot match all of
them.

11
00:00:45,407 --> 00:00:48,836
Could we just split the vector into
blocks?

12
00:00:49,636 --> 00:00:55,059
A contiguous split puts smooth and
unstable dimensions in the same group.

13
00:00:55,859 --> 00:01:00,144
Can we learn a representation that groups
them by behaviour?

14
00:01:00,394 --> 00:01:04,531
LinCa learns an invertible mapping of the
cached feature.

15
00:01:05,331 --> 00:01:08,611
It rearranges the dimensions into three
groups.

16
00:01:09,411 --> 00:01:12,691
The first group collects the unstable
dimensions.

17
00:01:12,941 --> 00:01:16,221
The second collects those that evolve
smoothly.

18
00:01:16,471 --> 00:01:19,751
The third collects the gently curved ones.

19
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:24,567
Three groups is the design choice used in
the paper.

20
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:29,304
Each group now gets a predictor that fits
it.

21
00:01:30,104 --> 00:01:34,670
The unstable group is simply reused from
the nearest cache.

22
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:39,057
The smoother groups use Hermite
extrapolation at matched order.

23
00:01:39,307 --> 00:01:43,016
Then the inverse mapping reconstructs the
full feature.

24
00:01:43,266 --> 00:01:46,357
Same feature, different subspaces,
different rules.

25
00:01:46,957 --> 00:01:49,529
Why must the mapping be invertible?

26
00:01:50,329 --> 00:01:53,609
So the decomposition itself loses no
information.

27
00:01:53,859 --> 00:01:57,567
Each block mixes channels and couples two
halves.

28
00:01:57,817 --> 00:02:02,503
One half updates the other, and then the
roles swap.

29
00:02:02,753 --> 00:02:05,604
Every step has an explicit inverse.

30
00:02:05,854 --> 00:02:11,129
The prediction can still be wrong; only
the mapping is exact.

31
00:02:11,729 --> 00:02:15,437
The denoising trajectory is also split
into stages.

32
00:02:16,237 --> 00:02:20,374
Each stage trains its own copy of the
predictor.

33
00:02:20,624 --> 00:02:23,904
That is separate from the subspace
decomposition.

34
00:02:24,704 --> 00:02:27,984
Training uses a few hundred pre-generated
features.

35
00:02:28,234 --> 00:02:32,651
One loss matches the feature; another
matches each component.

36
00:02:32,901 --> 00:02:36,273
Decompose, predict, reconstruct: that is
LinCa.
